    "body": "Most people know Dwayne Johnson as an actor who appears in action movies and family adventures. Fans of professional wrestling also know him as The Rock. But his original dream involved a different sport: football. Dwayne played college football before turning toward wrestling and eventually acting. His career demonstrates that an early plan can change while the skills learned along the way remain useful.\n\n## Football as a first goal\n\nDwayne grew up in a family connected to professional wrestling, but he also worked seriously at football. He played for the University of Miami, where being part of a competitive college program meant practicing, studying plays, and earning a position on the team. Football required physical training and a willingness to accept coaching. Not every player becomes a professional star, and Dwayne eventually had to reconsider what kind of career he wanted.\n\n## Creating The Rock\n\nAfter football, he entered professional wrestling. Wrestling entertainment combines athletic movement with planned characters and dramatic storylines. The performers are skilled, but the events are not organized like an ordinary unscripted sports competition. Dwayne developed the personality of The Rock, a confident character known for speaking directly to audiences. Speaking in front of a crowd can be frightening, but repeated performances help people learn timing and how to communicate clearly.\n\n## Moving into film\n\nDwayne later began acting in movies, appearing in action films and family entertainment. His projects include Jumanji: Welcome to the Jungle and the Disney animated movie Moana, in which he voiced Maui. Voice acting is different from appearing on camera because the performer must communicate emotion through sound. A character might be smiling, nervous, or joking, and the audience needs to understand that through the words and delivery.\n\n## What skills traveled with him?\n\nFootball taught Dwayne to work as part of a team and prepare for competition. Wrestling helped him develop a stage presence and adjust when a live audience responded unexpectedly. Film required new skills such as working with cameras, repeating scenes, and cooperating with directors. None of those fields is identical, yet each uses practice and communication. A career change does not necessarily mean throwing away everything learned before.\n\n## A role is not a whole person\n\nIt is important to separate an entertainer from the fictional characters they perform. A dramatic wrestling persona is a planned part of a show, not a complete description of the actor's private life. In the same way, a movie hero can do impossible things because filmmakers create those scenes with effects and stunt teams. Media literacy means enjoying entertainment while understanding how it was made.\n\n## Think about changing plans\n\nWrite down one activity you enjoy today and three skills it helps you practice. A student who likes basketball might build teamwork, planning, and persistence. Someone who likes drawing may learn observation and patience. Even if your future job is different, those skills may still be valuable. Dwayne Johnson's path from sports to acting shows how experiences can connect in ways a person did not expect at the beginning.",
